    "title": "5 classic Cleveland albums every vinyl collector should own - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Troy Smith ,\n17 hours ago \"Eternally thugsta, East 1999!\" Photo: Troy Smith/Axios There is no shortage of Cleveland-related records for vinyl lovers to add to their collections.\nHere are five albums every local music fan should look to get their hands on:\nBone Thugs-n-Harmony – \"E. 1999 Eternal\" (1995) 🎙️ Bone Thugs' second album put the group from Cleveland's eastside on the national hip-hop map.\nThe version pictured above is a special \"red-n-white\" colorway released by the subscription service Vinyl Me Please in 2023.\nNine Inch Nails – \"Pretty Hate Machine\" (1989) 🎛️ Trent Reznor recorded parts of NIN's debut album at Cleveland's The Right Track studio.\nIf you can't get your hands on the original TVT Records pressing , the 2011 reissue is a solid option. Pere Ubu – \"The Modern Dance\" (1978) 🤘 Pere Ubu's debut album, recorded at Cleveland Recording Company and Painesville's Suma Recording Studio, was a landmark of the post-punk era.\nThe album has been reissued by Fire Records in standard black and a special white colorway . Michael Stanley Band – \"Stagepass\" (1977) 🎟️ MSB's music was never experienced better than in a live setting, as evident by the recording of the band's 1977 show at Agora.\nYou'll have to search hard for a quality version of the original double-LP, but it's worth it. Raspberries – \"Raspberries\" (1972) 🎹 The Raspberries, who rose out of the ashes of local bands the Choir and Cyrus Erie, made their mark on the national power-pop scene with the band's debut album on Capitol Records.\nThe Sound of Vinyl released a limited, red-vinyl version in 2017. 2",
    "summary": "Photo: Troy Smith/Axios There is no shortage of Cleveland-related records for vinyl lovers to add to their collections. The version pictured above is a special \"red-n-white\" colorway released by the subscription service Vinyl Me Please in 2023. Nine Inch Nails – \"Pretty Hate Machine\" (1989) 🎛️ Trent Reznor recorded parts of NIN's debut album at Cleveland's The Right Track studio. If you can't get your hands on the original TVT Records pressing , the 2011 reissue is a solid option. The album has been reissued by Fire Records in standard black and a special white colorway .",
